Architecture note: the web component does nav-listening AND DOM scroll ops in one place (it reads
the global #main-content element). SwiftUI cannot query a global scroll view, so the surface is split
into two pieces sharing one model — the transparent `ScrollRestoration(model:)` companion (drives the
nav seam) + the `.scrollRestoration(model)` modifier (saves the offset + applies the restore). This is
the faithful native parity of one web component.

Leaf-state → web-branch mapping (no fabricated network states for a local utility):
  preparing   ← before the first navigation resolves                 (loading peer)
  restored    ← POP with a finite saved offset → return to it         (data/loaded peer)
  freshTop    ← PUSH / REPLACE → start at the top                     (empty peer)
  noSavedTop  ← POP with nothing saved yet → start at the top         (empty peer)
  unavailable ← session store disabled → can't persist; still tops    (error/offline peer)

